  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe aim to improve the C\u2085 bound by optimizing the step function h using gradient-based methods. The initial_h_values provide a good starting point. Our approach focuses on minimizing the maximum overlap integral while respecting constraints. We use scipy's optimization tools for their efficiency and ability to handle nonlinear problems with constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- **Variables**: h \u2208 [0,1]^n with sum(h) = n/2.\n- **Objective**: Minimize max_k { (h * (1 - roll(h, k))) }, linearly scaled by dx.\n- **Constraints**: 0 \u2264 h \u2264 1, sum(h) = n/2.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- **Initialization**: Use initial_h_values as the starting vector.\n- **Define Objective**: Compute overlap for all lags, return the maximum scaled by dx.\n- **Gradient Calculation**: Use finite differences for simplicity given time constraints.\n- **Optimization**: Use scipy.optimize.minimize with L-BFGS-B, handling constraints via projection.\n- **Time Management**: Check time periodically, halt if budget exceeded.\n  \n4. Search Choices\n- **Starting Point**: initial_h_values\n- **Optimization Method**: L-BFGS-B for efficiency\n- **Constraint Handling**: Project to feasible region after each iteration\n- **Evaluation Points**: Vary discretization level from 70 to 100 to check robustness\n\n5. Compute Budget\n- **Gradient Calculations**: ~1e-5s per call, negligible\n- **Time Per Iteration**: Dominated by objective eval (~1ms)\n- **Max Iterations**: Aim for 1e4, adjusting based on available time\n\n6. Validation\n- **Feasibility**: Ensure sum(h) = n/2 \u00b1 1e-6 and h in [0,1]\n- **Objective**: Recompute C5 after optimization",
